robots V3.0 -- Paul J. Condie, 1986-87.  Source for CMDS/GAMES/robots.WHERE IT CAME FROM   An OS-9 `ar` archive found outside this collection.  Its first sector had   been lost -- 256 bytes of $E5, RBF's fill for an unallocated sector -- so   `ar` refused it as "file not archive or damaged".  Skipping the dead sector   recovers 31 of the 32 members.  The one casualty was AppendNode.c.WHAT WAS CHANGED, AND WHY   AppendNode.c   RECONSTRUCTED, not recovered.  Its contract is fixed by its                  two callers -- TopTen.c and loadtops.c both append, test the                  result against 0, and then use *tail -- so the body is not                  guesswork, but it is not the author's code either.  Written                  to mirror InsertNode.c.   define.h       `typedef int void;` disabled.  It was a shim for a compiler                  with no void type; Microware cc has one, and the line is now                  a syntax error.  The only use of void in the tree is                  `void srand();` in Main.c, which is valid with the real type.   GameBoard.c    the two attrset calls around the border were commented out                  in this source revision, so the arena border did not draw                  at all -- BORDERCHAR is a SPACE, and without an attribute                  a space is nothing.  Restored, but as A_STANDOUT, NOT                  A_REVERSE: /dd/DEFS/curses.h defines A_REVERSE, A_BOLD,                  A_DIM, A_BLINK and A_UNDERLINE all as 0, so attrset of any                  of them CLEARS attributes rather than setting one.  Only                  A_STANDOUT (W_STANDOUT) is real; it emits the termcap                  so/se.  The rebuilt binary now emits the same 48 ESC[7m/                  ESC[27m the archive binary did.  Note TopTen.c still uses                  A_REVERSE and A_BOLD to highlight your own entry -- those                  are no-ops here for the same reason, left alone.   getdate.c      `time.t_year % 100`.  t_year is years since 1900, so the                  top-ten list printed 2026 as "126".   os9stuff.c     getlogin() returned the literal "your name", so every                  score in the top-ten files was recorded under it -- the                  entries shipped with the game, dated 1987, all read that                  way, and so did every new one.  It now takes USER from                  the environment (SYS/login sets it), then LOGNAME, then                  the user number as group.user; eight characters, the                  width of the column.  The shipped score files are left                  as they were.  2026-09-10.BUILDING   The makefile's own `make` does not complete -- it has explicit rules for   most objects but not all, and OS-9 make has no implicit .c to .r rule to   fall back on.  Drive cc directly:       setenv CLIB /dd/LIB       setenv CDEF /dd/DEFS       cc -qgxt=/dd -r=RELS <every .c>       chd RELS       cc -qgxm=16k <every .r> -l=<LIB>/curses.l -l=<LIB>/termlib.l \          -l=<LIB>/math.l -l=<LIB>/clib.l -f=robots   math.l is required and the makefile omits it: curses.l refers to _T$LtoD,   _T$DtoL and _T$DMul, which live in the Math trap module.  Without it the   link stops with "unresolved references".WHY THE BINARY HERE WAS REPLACED   The archive binary drew a cursor-up as a bare ^K (0x0B).  A terminal reads   that byte as index -- cursor DOWN -- so a run of them walked off the bottom   of the screen and scrolled it, and everything drawn afterwards landed on   shifted lines.  In play it looked like characters left behind that were not   really there, getting worse the longer a game ran.  Rebuilding from this   source against the current curses.l produces a binary that does not emit   the byte at all: 3400 moves across two long sessions, audited every move   against the game's own robot counter, with no disagreement.PLAYING   robots -m   manual mode: the robots take one full step per move you make.   Keys are the numeric keypad, 1-9.  5 stands still -- it is accepted but   moves nobody, so the robots take another step while you do not.   s  last stand: stop taking input and let them come.   t  teleport.   Walking onto a robot or a junk heap kills you; the border only stops you.   Two robots meeting leave a heap and score double; one hitting a heap dies.   A screen is worth about 400n^2, so the score follows how far you survive.